            <div xml:id="d1556">

                <pb facs="0995" n="879" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg879"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 312.51/80.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        Vice Consul Silliman.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-08-04T21:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 4, 1914—9 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>We are informed three Christian Brothers, the President, Adrien Astruc, Professor
                    Adolphus Giles, both of them French, and Father Vega, Mexican, were shot June
                    24, 4 p.m., by order of General Chao. Fourteen others banished, and now at Santa
                    Fe, New Mexico, having been forced to make a payment of $5,000 ransom. By order
                    of the same General, 22 priests Zacatecas threatened death unless they paid
                    hundred thousand dollars, and upon payment they were arrested and banished, and
                    sent to El Paso.</p>
                <p>Lay the above before General Carranza and again impress upon him the seriousness
                    of these acts and urgently insist that the parties guilty be adequately
                    punished.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bryan</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
